C. MB inequality hall(T) <= min_F sum_i MB(P, C_i) over spanning 1-path-cycle factors F (>= 1 cycle)
==============================================================================
N=3: 2 classes; no 1-path-cycle factor with a cycle: 2; min_F sum MB < hall (merge count alone insufficient): 0 (max deficit 0); == hall: 0
N=4: 4 classes; no 1-path-cycle factor with a cycle: 1; min_F sum MB < hall (merge count alone insufficient): 0 (max deficit 0); == hall: 0
N=5: 12 classes; no 1-path-cycle factor with a cycle: 1; min_F sum MB < hall (merge count alone insufficient): 0 (max deficit 0); == hall: 3
N=6: 56 classes; no 1-path-cycle factor with a cycle: 1; min_F sum MB < hall (merge count alone insufficient): 3 (max deficit 1); == hall: 14
N=7: 456 classes; no 1-path-cycle factor with a cycle: 1; min_F sum MB < hall (merge count alone insufficient): 65 (max deficit 3); == hall: 135
witness: T = 110010111001110101111 (N=7, hall=6); F = path (2, 4, 6, 1) + cycles [(0, 5, 3)]; sum MB = 3
  D_F = [(0, 2), (1, 3), (3, 6)] (|D_F| = 3) blocks every simple merge of F: True; yet T - D_F has the Hamiltonian path (0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6)

